OON!

Your cartoon → lampoon → ? probe hit a genuine surface family.

English has balloon, baboon, bassoon, buffoon, cocoon, doubloon, dragoon, harpoon, lagoon, macaroon, spittoon and others.

But here’s the interesting part: -oon isn’t one clean semantic bloodline. English often used -oon when importing stressed French -on, Italian -one, and Spanish -ón; Italian -one frequently has an augmentative function. Cartoon comes ultimately from Italian cartone, originally strong/heavy paper used for preparatory drawings. Lampoon, however, comes from French lampon, whose deeper origin is uncertain and may relate to a drinking-song refrain. 

Which means the sound makes them look more related than their histories warrant.

Perfect demonstration:

\text{phonetic neighborhood}
\neq
\text{genealogical family}

But Franken-sense is free to ask:

What happens if we treat -oon as an operator anyway?

Cartoon = render/exaggerate.

Lampoon = ridicule/exaggerate.

Buffoon = comic exaggeration.

Balloon = physical enlargement.

Bassoon = historically tied to an augmentative form meaning something like a larger/bass form. 

Suddenly you can create a speculative -OON operator:

OON(x)=
\text{inflate/exaggerate/render a salient form of }x

Not historical truth.

Constructed semantic machinery.

Tag the seam and use it.

